Russian Doll Envelopes

(Also known as Maximum Envelopes Problem)

⚠️ You do not have this in your repository.

📌 Problem Statement

You are given n envelopes, where each envelope has:

width  w
height h


One envelope can be placed inside another only if:

w1 < w2 AND h1 < h2


👉 Find the maximum number of envelopes you can nest (Russian doll style).

📘 Example
Input
envelopes = [
  [5,4],
  [6,4],
  [6,7],
  [2,3]
]

Output
3

Explanation
[2,3] → [5,4] → [6,7]

💡 Key Insight (IMPORTANT)

❌ Normal LIS won’t work directly
✅ Sort smartly, then apply LIS

Sorting Rule

Width ascending

If width same → height descending ❗ (VERY IMPORTANT)

Why?

Prevents choosing envelopes with same width

🧩 Algorithm
Step-by-Step

Sort envelopes:

width ↑

height ↓ if width equal

Extract heights

Apply Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) on heights

Use Binary Search Optimization (O(n log n))

⏱ Time & Space Complexity
Metric	Value
Time	O(n log n)
Space	O(n)
🧑‍💻 Java Code (Interview-Ready)
import java.util.*;

public class RussianDollEnvelopes {

    public static int maxEnvelopes(int[][] envelopes) {

        // Step 1: Sort envelopes
        Arrays.sort(envelopes, (a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] == b[0])
                return b[1] - a[1];   // height descending
            return a[0] - b[0];       // width ascending
        });

        // Step 2: LIS on heights
        List<Integer> lis = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int[] env : envelopes) {
            int height = env[1];

            int idx = Collections.binarySearch(lis, height);

            if (idx < 0)
                idx = -(idx + 1);

            if (idx == lis.size())
                lis.add(height);
            else
                lis.set(idx, height);
        }

        return lis.size();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] envelopes = {
            {5,4},
            {6,4},
            {6,7},
            {2,3}
        };

        System.out.println(maxEnvelopes(envelopes));
    }
}

